<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sermon on Law and Grace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1037–1050</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sermon on Law and Grace is a work by Hilarion, later metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, composed between about 1037 and 1050. Contrasting the Law of the Old Testament with the grace of the New, it celebrates the conversion of Rus' and praises <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, placing the Rus' among the Christian nations. It is regarded as the first masterpiece of East Slavic literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sermon-on-law-and-grace.jpg" alt="Sermon on Law and Grace" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shakko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torzhestvennik_with_Sermon_on_Law_and_Grace_(15th_c.,_GIM)_by_shakko.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
